WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:06.240
In less than 30 years, Amazon went from selling&nbsp;
books to selling everything you can imagine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:12.080
Opening up grocery stores, offering a selection of&nbsp;
movies and TV shows, and one day, plans to launch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:17.040
a satellite internet service. The company's&nbsp;
growth has been nothing short of stunning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:23.600
In 1995 - the year the site went live - it made half&nbsp;
a million dollars in sales. In 2020 - that soared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:33.040
to $386 billion. So how did Amazon get so big? By&nbsp;
following one simple rule over and over again. 

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:39.840
Customer obsession is the key to the success of&nbsp;
Amazon - or amazon.com - as it was known at the start.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:45.120
That focus on the customer led Jeff Bezos to start&nbsp;
off by selling books. He believed that would appeal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:50.000
to people the most as he explained during the&nbsp;
company's early days. There are more items in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.120
book category than there are items in any other&nbsp;
category by far. A web store would not be limited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:59.600
by the physical constraints of a bookstore so&nbsp;
customers could quickly and easily get their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:04.560
hands on whatever they wanted like this title&nbsp;
about computer modeling - the first book the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:10.240
ever sold. In those early days, Bezos and a small&nbsp;
team packaged and mailed off the orders themselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:16.000
Other online bookstores existed at the time so&nbsp;
Amazon had to find a way to set itself apart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.600
by adding user-generated reviews. Again, the company&nbsp;
thought about what would be best for the customer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.960
though not necessarily the publishing house. Bezos&nbsp;
once recalled receiving an unhappy letter from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:31.520
publisher about the feature. One wrote to me&nbsp;
and said, "You don't understand your business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:35.760
you make money when you sell things. Why do&nbsp;
you allow these negative customer reviews?"

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.680
And when I read that letter, I thought, we don't&nbsp;
make money when we sell things. We make money when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:47.520
we help customers make purchase decisions. In other&nbsp;
words, Amazon stuck to its core principle: build the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:54.320
best customer experience possible which extended&nbsp;
to more features. In 1999, the company patented the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:59.840
1-Click button which lets you skip the shopping&nbsp;
cart by purchasing any item quickly and easily as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:05.040
website remembers your previously entered payment&nbsp;
and address information. The patent lasted all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:11.680
way until 2017. When Barnes &amp; Noble implemented a&nbsp;
similar feature, Amazon sued - forcing the bookseller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:17.200
to add an extra step at checkout. Some argue that&nbsp;
patents during the tech boom of the 90s were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:23.520
too broad and too powerful. 1-Click was a huge&nbsp;
asset for the company. It needed any advantage it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:29.520
could get when the dot-com bubble burst, causing&nbsp;
Amazon to lose over 90 per cent of its valuation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:34.720
It went from a split adjusted initial public&nbsp;
offering of a $1.50 a share in 1997&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:43.680
to $106.69 cents a share in December 1999 before&nbsp;
falling all the way back down to $5.97 a share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:50.240
in September 2001. Unlike many other tech firms,&nbsp;
Amazon managed to survive by raising a ton of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:55.520
money just before the market crashed. During&nbsp;
those turbulent years, Bezos made moves that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:02.400
would define his company - chief among them: lowering&nbsp;
prices. In the early 2000s, Amazon decided to cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:08.560
the price of some items by 30 or 40 per cent in&nbsp;
some cases. Its thinking went something like this:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:14.000
low prices attract more customers which increases&nbsp;
sales which attracts more third-party sellers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.800
who pay a commission and store their goods&nbsp;
at Amazon's warehouses for faster delivery.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:22.880
Since more items are now going through these&nbsp;
fulfillment centers which have fixed fees like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:28.640
the lease, this lowers the per unit cost which&nbsp;
saves the company more money - enabling it to lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:35.200
prices even further. And the cycle repeats. There&nbsp;
was just one hurdle to overcome. Amazon had started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:40.960
to diversify beyond books but was having trouble&nbsp;
convincing customers to purchase items in multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:47.360
categories so it needed to offer an incentive.&nbsp;
Amazon realized that one of the biggest barriers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:53.920
to shopping online was the expensive shipping so&nbsp;
it introduced Free Super Saver Shipping in 2002&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:59.760
for those who spent over $99, then it kept lowering&nbsp;
the minimum purchase threshold, first to $49&nbsp;

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:05.200
then $25. Customers started&nbsp;
to fill their virtual baskets with more products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:12.320
from different categories - setting the stage in&nbsp;
2005 for Amazon Prime, forever changing the way we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:18.320
shop. Prime members could receive unlimited two-day&nbsp;
shipping on all orders for a flat annual fee of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:25.440
$79. The math didn't make much sense. Amazon would in&nbsp;
fact lose money. But Bezos bet the membership would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:31.040
prompt customers to buy more and that's exactly&nbsp;
what happened. Customers signed up like crazy -&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:36.080
further expanding Amazon's market share. As&nbsp;
Brad Stone details in his book about the company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:40.880
an Amazon executive likened signing up for Prime&nbsp;
as "...going from a dial up to a broadband internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:46.640
connection." Two-day shipping eventually led to&nbsp;
one day and same-day shipping and now, Amazon is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:52.720
developing a drone service that aims to deliver&nbsp;
packages in just 30 minutes. Ironically, after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:57.920
creating a global retail giant online, the company&nbsp;
is now opening brick and mortar stores with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:03.840
twist. You never have to wait in line. At amazon&nbsp;
Go convenience stores, shoppers walk in, swipe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:08.880
their phone like a boarding pass at the airport,&nbsp;
while AI-powered sensors track their purchase and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:13.600
automatically charge their credit card via the&nbsp;
Amazon app. There's no need to go to a cashier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:19.840
or a self-check kiosk. However, such rapid and&nbsp;
continual growth doesn't come without criticism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:23.840
There have been allegations in the past that&nbsp;
workers in the warehouses were so exhausted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:29.440
they had panic attacks or fell asleep on their&nbsp;
feet. The firm is also accused of acting like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:36.560
monopoly. When launching the Kindle in 2007, Amazon&nbsp;
decided to price electronic books at just $9.99, &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:42.080
hoping to tilt the book business toward e-reading&nbsp;
by offering customers a steal. Publishers suspected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:47.120
the low price would hurt them as Amazon could use&nbsp;
its market power to then demand cheaper wholesale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:52.960
prices. In a famous dispute over pricing, Amazon&nbsp;
temporarily took away the buy button on titles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:58.160
from the publisher Macmillan, only offering them&nbsp;
through outside vendors. Despite accusations of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:03.840
brutal bullying tactics, the goal of Amazon has&nbsp;
always been to pass on more savings to customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:10.560
and customers are responding. Amazon delivered&nbsp;
a record performance in 2020 with net sales&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:16.720
increasing 38 per cent compared to the year before as&nbsp;
the pandemic led people to buy more online.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:20.560
The secret sauce of Amazon, where there are&nbsp;
several principles at Amazon, but the number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:28.080
one thing that has made us successful by far is&nbsp;
obsessive-compulsive focus on the customer as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:34.080
opposed to obsession over the competitor. There's&nbsp;
a great interview on CuriosityStream that gives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:39.840
an inside look into Jeff Bezos' mindset and what&nbsp;
made his company a stunning success. You can watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

